# SonarQube Code Quality

- Prefer simple, readable code: small functions, early returns, and shallow nesting.
- Remove duplication before adding code; extract shared hooks, helpers, constants, or components.
- Keep branching easy to follow; avoid repeated conditions, nested ternaries, and dense boolean expressions.
- Handle errors explicitly; never leave empty `catch` blocks or silently ignore failures.
- Use precise types in TypeScript; avoid `any`, unsafe casts, and non-null assertions unless clearly justified.
- Keep React render paths pure; no side effects in render, correct hook dependencies, and stable list keys.
- Delete dead code, unreachable branches, unused variables, and commented-out implementations instead of leaving them behind.
- Validate nullable or external data at boundaries; prefer safe access and guard clauses over assumptions.
- Avoid suppressing lint or quality rules; if suppression is unavoidable, keep it narrow and explain why.
- Leave touched code clearer, safer, and easier to test than before.

## Quick examples

```ts
// Bad
if (user && user.profile && user.profile.name) save(user.profile.name);

// Good
const profileName = user?.profile?.name;
if (!profileName) return;
save(profileName);
```

```ts
// Bad
try {
  await submit();
} catch (error) {}

// Good
try {
  await submit();
} catch (error) {
  reportError(error);
  throw error;
}
```
